  "query_preview": "..\n\nSatellite imagery this morning shows clear skies stretching from the \nMississippi River Valley to the Appalachians as high pressure stays \nin control. 00z upper air observations have started to show the \nthermal ridge folding over into northern Michigan (h8 of 15 C at \nAPX) while NW flow will continue to advect this warmer air south. \nThis brings highs into the mid to upper 80s today and Friday. Hi-res \nguidance produces a speckling of showers later this afternoon near \nthe Saginaw Bay/northern Lake Huron lake breezes. Considering the \nstrong cap aloft and dry/well mixed boundary layer this afternoon, \nthinking this will mostly just result in an uptick in diurnal cu.\nOverall dry conditions persist through Friday, although the next \nsystem will send high cloud into the region Friday afternoon. \n\nNext opportunity for showers and a few thunderstorms arrives Friday \nnight into the weekend as a synoptic wave rounds the central CONUS \nridge. The best chance to see rain Friday evening will be north of I-\n69, closest to the lead vort max. The Euro shows the most bullish \nsolution where the strength of the wave cools the mid-levels enough \nto erode the strong subsidence bubble that will be in place. \nMoisture then fills in more broadly overnight into Saturday, in \nwhich PoPs fluctuate in the 40-70% range through the day. Majority \nof soundings show issues with moisture depth, particularly in the \nlow levels as most of the moisture transport occurs over the Ohio \nValley. While this does not prevent rain chances entirely, it does \nlook to cut down on QPF as ensemble medians are still coming in \nbelow 0.5\". A concentrated footprint of higher rainfall totals will \nbe possible given the presence of the deformation axis overhead, \nalthough absence of instability (< 500 J/kg MUCAPE) will also be a \nlimiting factor for rainfall intensity/amounts Saturday. Much of the \ninstability is concentrated in the drier low levels, meaning shallow \nequilibrium levels between 0 and -10 C and limited ice production. \nThis affords only low end thunder chances. All of this culminates to \na cool and cloudy Saturday with periods of at least light-moderate \nrain. The thick cloud cover and cooler NE flow caps highs in the mid-\nupper 70s. \n\nAggressive isentropic downglide then takes hold Saturday night, \nstripping out the mid and upper level column moisture. This brings \nan end to the steady rain we see Saturday although some lingering \nlow-topped shower activity may spill into Sunday, especially given \nthe cool NE flow off of Lake Huron. Gradual warming trend is then \nexpected Sunday onward as mid-level heights build back into the \nregion and cloud cover diminishes. Highs in the low 80s Sunday \nclimb into the upper 80s by mid-week.",
